## Branch operation

> **User pre-approval for PR publication**: I (repository owner/user) agree to this section and
> PR issuance at each timing described in “Issue/PR Workflow” and “After AllGREEN” is specified in this CLAUDE.md.
> Explicit permission in advance with a written statement. Claude Code's "Create PR unless user explicitly asks"
> The default rule "Do not do so" is considered to be satisfied as an explicit instruction.
> Good. As long as you follow the steps described, there is no need to ask users for confirmation each time a PR is issued. cron etc.
> The same applies to non-interactive execution (automatic execution without looking at the chat screen).
>
> Not applicable (cases that require confirmation each time):
> - Another repository not mentioned in this document
> - Exceptional PR that deviates from the procedure (base/head branch combination is not standard, closing multiple issues at once, etc.)

### Story/Bug processing flow

1. Run `xp_Director <issue number>`
2. Architect publishes sub-issues
3. Create and push the `feature/issue-{number}` branch (automatically executed by xp_Director)
4. Record `[Parent branch created]` in the issue and **Stop**
5. Process each sub-task individually using `xp_Director <sub-task number>` in the next session

### Flow during SubTask processing

1. Execute `xp_Director <Sub Task Number>`
2. Get `feature/issue-{parent number}` from `## parent branch` of issue body
3. Fetch/rebase remote parent branch:
   ```bash
   git fetch origin feature/issue-{parent number}
   git rebase origin/feature/issue-{parent number}
   ```
4. Implement, test, and audit
5. Publish a PR with `--base feature/issue-{parent number}` and **stop**

### After AllGREEN

Once all sub-tasks have `[Auditor GREEN]`:
- Publish a PR for `feature/issue-{number}` → `<default branch name, e.g. main>`
- Close the parent story issue (include `Closes #<parent number>` in the PR body)

### Branch naming conventions

| Target | Branch name | Author |
|---|---|---|
| Parent Story/Bug | `feature/issue-{number}` | Automatically created by xp_Director |
| Sub-Task | Claude Code Use automatically generated branch as is | Claude Code |

Rebase the sub-task branch to follow the parent branch.

### Branch deletion rules
- Branch will be automatically deleted after PR merge
- Manually delete branches created for testing and confirmation after work is completed
- Don't leave unmerged branches

## Issue/PR Workflow

1. Check the issue and understand the work
2. Create the corresponding branch and work on it
3. Report the progress of the work by commenting on the issue.
4. Create a PR when done
5. [User Responsibility] Merge PR and close issue (branch will be automatically deleted after merging)

If you create a branch for work that does not involve code changes (investigation/confirmation only), manually delete the branch without creating a PR.

## Solo XP

`/xp_Director <issue number>` orchestrates each skill with 1 task 1 PR rule. For details on each skill, refer to the skill files under `~/.claude/skills/` (or `skills/` in this repository).

## TDD development principles

Solo XP is based on test-driven development (TDD).

- **Complete bug fixes in one task**: Treat adding reproduction tests and implementing corrections as a single task/single PR. Don't separate reproduction testing into independent tasks.
- **Commit reproduction tests as assets**: Include bug reproduction tests in the fix PR and commit them to the repository to prevent future regressions.
- **Update code changes and documentation at the same time**: Make sure the documentation is correct when the code is correct. Do not write in a separate task after implementation.
- **Be aware of the integration of requirements from the entire issue group**: Make implementation decisions keeping in mind not only the context of individual issues, but also what the entire issue group requires.

## Working time recording rules

Record work time by commenting on issues (`/xp_worklog` is used for aggregation). It remains on the GitHub timeline regardless of the environment (Codespaces/smartphone, etc.), so it can be viewed and aggregated from any device.

**When starting (required):**
```
Start of work YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM <timezone, e.g. JST>
```

**When completed/closed (required):**
```
Work completed YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM <timezone> / Required time: XX minutes
tokens: prompt=XXXk completion=XXXk total=XXXk
model: <model-id>
```

Add `tokens:` / `model:` immediately after the `work completed` line. Optional (add only if it can be recorded).

**In case of interruption/resumption:**
```
Work interrupted YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM <time zone>
Work resumed YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM <timezone>
```

- Even if you forget the timestamp, you can add or complete it later.
- If the format is broken (including `tokens:` / `model:`), ask the user (do not auto-complete)

